---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { release } from '../data/releases';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy & local-first | Slate"
  description="How Slate Desktop and its browser extension process captures locally, store your library on your computer, and handle privacy."
  home={false}
>
  <article class="shell privacy-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">SLATE / PRIVACY</p>
    <h1>
      Your memory.
      <br />
      <span>On your computer.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="privacy-intro">
      Slate is local-first: your captures stay on your computer, not in a Slate
      cloud library. This policy covers Slate Desktop for Windows, its Chrome
      extension, and this website. Current releases are Desktop{' '}
      {release.version} and extension 0.1.1. No Slate account is required.
    </p>
    <section>
      <h2>Local storage, not a cloud library</h2>
      <p>
        Clips, groups, and source metadata are stored in a local plaintext
        SQLite database. Slate handles this information locally so you can find
        and reuse it; local storage does not mean no data is processed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The database is not encrypted by Slate. Windows account security, device
        encryption, filesystem access, and backups remain your responsibility.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>You choose what to capture</h2>
      <p>
        Save or Save selection saves text you choose, including personal
        communications from AI conversations such as ChatGPT, Claude, and
        Gemini. Slate does not automatically collect complete conversation
        histories. Save this page stores the current HTTP/HTTPS URL and page
        title as a Link clip, not the entire page text or HTML.
      </p>
      <p>
        Saved records can include content, type, source application or
        classification, source URL, page title, a locally generated ID,
        timestamps, and pin or group metadata. URLs and titles record the source
        of your chosen captures. Slate does not read, enumerate, or continuously
        monitor your browser history.
      </p>
      <p>
        Selected text, page titles, and URLs may contain sensitive information,
        including URL query parameters or fragments. Slate does not
        automatically detect or redact that information. Choose what you save
        carefully.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>The floating Save control</h2>
      <p>
        The floating Save content script runs only on https://chatgpt.com/*,
        https://chat.openai.com/*, https://claude.ai/*, and
        https://gemini.google.com/*. It is not injected across every website.
      </p>
      <p>
        To show and position the control, it temporarily processes your selected
        text, selection geometry, current document URL, and relevant pointer,
        keyboard-selection, focus, and navigation state locally. It keeps no
        click, keystroke, pointer, or interaction history. The selection
        snapshot is discarded when the control is dismissed. Merely selecting
        text sends nothing to the native host; an explicit trusted Save action
        is required to save it to your library.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Chrome permissions</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>activeTab:</strong> Temporary access after a user action to
        relevant active-tab information, such as the source URL and title.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>contextMenus:</strong> Adds Save selection and Save this page
        browser actions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>nativeMessaging:</strong> Sends user-initiated captures to the
        installed Slate native host on the same computer for local storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>notifications:</strong> Shows local capture success or failure
        feedback. The limited supported-site content scripts are described
        above. The extension uses no remotely hosted JavaScript, WebAssembly, or
        runtime-downloaded executable code.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Local capture communication</h2>
      <p>
        A browser capture passes through the extension's background service
        worker and Chrome Native Messaging to the installed native host. The
        payload contains content, content type, source classification, URL, and
        page title. The host writes directly to local SQLite using Slate's
        shared persistence implementation, not through the desktop window. This
        is local process communication, not a cloud upload.
      </p>
      <p>
        Desktop selected-text capture uses a user-invoked Copy action and
        attempts to restore the previous clipboard state; some applications or
        clipboard formats can prevent capture or restoration.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Network and third parties</h2>
      <p>
        Slate does not upload your saved clips, source URLs, or browsing
        activity to Slate-operated or third-party servers as part of its capture
        and library functionality.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open source visits the stored URL in your browser. Visiting this
        website, GitHub, or source websites makes normal web requests. Copy
        places chosen content on the Windows clipboard, where other software or
        user-enabled clipboard services may access it. Windows, Chrome,
        WebView2, installation components, and other software have their own
        network and data-handling behavior outside Slate's control.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>No app analytics or telemetry</h2>
      <p>
        Slate has no account or authentication service, cloud sync, application
        analytics, or telemetry SDK. It does not upload usage events,
        diagnostics, or crash reports. This does not control telemetry from your
        browser, operating system, or other software.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Data retention and deletion</h2>
      <p>
        Clips stay in your local library until you delete them. Deleting a group
        can delete its member clips when you explicitly choose that operation.
        Unmerging removes the grouping, not the original clips. Ordinary
        uninstall retains the library; choosing Delete app data in the
        uninstaller removes the relevant application-data directories.
      </p>
      <p>
        Deletion is not a promise of secure forensic erasure. Deleted SQLite
        records, backups, or other copies may remain recoverable outside Slate's
        control. Keep a backup of information you need.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site saves your theme preference in browser localStorage. It has no
        account forms, analytics, tracking scripts, or application cookies. The
        hosting provider receives ordinary request information to serve the
        site; that is separate from Slate's local clip storage.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Data use and Chrome Limited Use</h2>
      <p>
        Slate does not sell user data or use captured information for
        advertising, creditworthiness, or lending. It does not use or transfer
        captured information for purposes unrelated to saving, finding, and
        reusing your local clips.
      </p>
      <p>
        Slate's use of information received from Chrome APIs complies with the
        Chrome Web Store User Data Policy, including its Limited Use
        requirements.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Privacy questions</h2>
      <p>
        Visit{' '}
        <a class="text-link" href="/support/">
          Slate Support
        </a>{' '}
        for the existing GitHub support channel. Do not put private clip
        content, sensitive URLs, or other personal information in public GitHub
        issues.
      </p>
    </section>
    <a class="text-link" href="/">
      Back to Slate <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
    </a>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
